  'thread',
  'Canada’s New Vehicle Sales Rose 7% as Zero-Emission Models Gained Share',
  'September sales volume advanced 7%, but a 66.6% increase in zero-emission vehicle purchases reshaped the market mix and prompted a closer look at household financing decisions.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>180,457 new vehicles were sold in September, up 7.0% year over year,</strong><span> according to Statistics Canada''s November 16 release.</span></li>
<li><strong>Sales dollars rose 10.8%, faster than vehicle counts,</strong><span> reflecting a change in the average sales value, not necessarily an equivalent increase in like-for-like prices.</span></li>
<li><strong>Zero-emission vehicle sales reached 28,490, a 66.6% annual increase,</strong><span> raising their share to 15.8% from 10.1% a year earlier.</span></li>
<li><strong>Passenger car sales gained 15.4% and truck sales 5.9%,</strong><span> as both broad vehicle categories recorded higher unit volumes.</span></li>
<li><strong>The ZEV share increased from 13.0% in August to 15.8% in September,</strong><span> though monthly unadjusted sales and the effect of incentives require careful interpretation.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Canada''s September new-vehicle sales report offered two signals rather than one. Unit sales recovered from a year earlier, but the shift in what consumers bought was more pronounced. Statistics Canada said on November 16 that dealers sold 180,457 new motor vehicles in September 2026, a 7.0% increase from the same month in 2025. Their combined sales value grew 10.8%. Within that expanding market, zero-emission vehicles gained a substantially larger share.</p>
<p>The agency counted 28,490 new zero-emission vehicles sold, 66.6% more than in September 2025. Those vehicles represented 15.8% of all new sales, compared with 10.1% a year earlier, a rise of 5.7 percentage points. The category includes battery-electric and plug-in hybrid vehicles, not conventional hybrids. That distinction matters when interpreting the transition in dealership demand.</p>
<h2>The Mix Shift Was Larger Than the Market''s Growth</h2>
<p>A 7.0% increase in overall vehicles sold indicates a firmer market than in September 2025, but ZEV unit sales rose at more than nine times that annual rate. The change in share is the clearer structural story: nearly one in six new vehicles sold in September had the potential for zero tailpipe emissions, compared with roughly one in ten a year earlier.</p>
<p>That result was not confined to a flat headline market. Sales of passenger cars increased 15.4% compared with September 2025, while sales of trucks grew 5.9%. These are broad vehicle classes; the release does not imply that every additional passenger car was electric or that ZEV demand alone caused total sales to rise.</p>
<p>Dollar sales rose faster than the count of vehicles. Dividing the reported 10.8% growth in total sales value by the 7.0% growth in units implies roughly 3.6% growth in average sales value per vehicle. That is an arithmetic description of this month''s mix of transactions, not a controlled vehicle-price index. More expensive models, different configurations and changing discount patterns can all influence such a comparison.</p>
<h2>Fifteen Months Show the Change in Share</h2>
<p>The longer monthly sequence gives September''s figure context. Statistics Canada''s reported ZEV sales share was 7.7% in July 2025, 8.8% in August, and about one-tenth in September. It moved unevenly through the winter, including 7.7% in January 2026, before returning to 10.2% in February and 12.2% in March. Following 9.6% in May, it climbed to 11.5% in June, 13.0% in August and 15.8% in September.</p>
