<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Zero-emission vehicles include battery-electric and plug-in hybrid vehicles. Monthly percentages reproduce Statistics Canada''s published rounded shares; the September 2025 comparison uses the latest 10.1% figure in the November 16, 2026 release rather than the original 10.2% publication. The shaded period marks the February 2026 federal incentive launch, not a causal estimate. Data are subject to revision.</p><p>The February 16 launch of the federal Electric Vehicle Affordability Program is a relevant policy date. Statistics Canada reported incentives of up to $5,000 for eligible battery-electric vehicles and $2,500 for plug-in hybrids at the time. The chart marks that launch, but it should not be read as proving that the program caused the rise. Consumer preferences, model availability, provincial policy, financing costs and purchase timing can also influence monthly sales.</p>
<h2>What the Figures Say for Household Planning</h2>
<p>For households, a vehicle purchase is both a transportation decision and a balance-sheet commitment. The useful comparison is not simply an electric vehicle''s sticker price against a gasoline model. It is the total financed amount, interest cost, estimated energy expense, insurance, servicing, expected ownership period and eventual resale value under assumptions appropriate to the household.</p>
<p>The financing calculation deserves particular care when total vehicle sales dollars are growing faster than unit counts. A higher transaction amount, even for a vehicle with lower operating costs, can increase monthly payment pressure. Buyers should also distinguish a government incentive they are actually eligible to receive from an advertised maximum, and confirm program terms at the date of purchase rather than assuming today''s conditions will continue unchanged.</p>
<p>For advisers, the September release is a prompt to revisit planned automobile spending, near-term cash reserves and major-debt decisions, not to forecast an uninterrupted acceleration in EV adoption. The statistics describe vehicles sold, not registrations, and are subject to revision. The clearest conclusion is that the Canadian auto market grew in September while zero-emission vehicles captured a materially larger portion of that market.</p>',
